WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.030 --> 00:00:01.680
INTRO:
Whether you watched every&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:01.680 --> 00:00:07.280
Disney and Pixar movie or you just tuned into&nbsp;
Phineas and Ferb here and there, for most of us,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.280 --> 00:00:10.520
Disney was a nostalgic part of our childhood. I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.040 --> 00:00:17.280
mean, who could forget their iconic theme&nbsp;
parks that are scattered all around the word.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.280 --> 00:00:21.920
While these factors are what most of us remember&nbsp;
about Disney, over the past few decades,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.920 --> 00:00:27.360
they’ve grown into basically every media industry&nbsp;
you can think. From Marvel movies and Star Wars to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.360 --> 00:00:33.920
Disney+ and ESPN, Disney owns much of the modern&nbsp;
media industry. This has put Disney in a pretty&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.920 --> 00:00:39.280
unique position as they can thrive it basically&nbsp;
any situation. If everyone is staying at home,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.280 --> 00:00:44.320
that’s perfect for Disney’s streaming services.&nbsp;
Conversely, if everyone is going out, that’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.320 --> 00:00:49.680
perfect for their theme parks and box office&nbsp;
numbers. Despite this unique advantage, Disney&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.680 --> 00:00:54.160
hasn’t been doing to well since the pandemic&nbsp;
started. In fact, their net income crashed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.160 --> 00:00:59.520
from roughly $10 billion per year to as low as&nbsp;
-$5 billion per year. They have since become&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.520 --> 00:01:04.080
profitable again, but their current net income&nbsp;
sits at just a fraction of their pre pandemic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.080 --> 00:01:09.680
numbers at $2-3 billion. Considering this, I don’t&nbsp;
think you’d be surprised to hear that their stock&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.680 --> 00:01:14.880
has gotten absolutely obliterated. Since their&nbsp;
peak in early 2021, Disney stock has fallen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.880 --> 00:01:21.920
right about 50% which correlates to a market cap&nbsp;
loss of $188 billion. So, here’s why Disney is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.920 --> 00:01:26.800
struggling on Wall Street despite owning a media&nbsp;
empire and giving Netflix a run for their money.

00:01:26.800 --> 00:01:31.520
UNDERWHELMING GROWTH:&nbsp;

00:01:31.520 --> 00:01:36.240
One of the biggest issues plaguing Disney is their&nbsp;
underwhelming growth atleast from the perspective&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.240 --> 00:01:42.640
of investors. Likely the most disappointing sector&nbsp;
for investors is Disney +. This is honestly quite&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.640 --> 00:01:47.840
ironic given that Disney+ has been killing it&nbsp;
eversince they launched. They’ve literally grown&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.840 --> 00:01:54.400
from 0 to 137.7 million subscribers within a&nbsp;
matter of 2.5 years. For perspective, it has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.400 --> 00:02:00.320
taken Netflix 15 years to grow to 200 million&nbsp;
subscribers. While this is phenomenal growth,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.320 --> 00:02:05.680
many investors are worried about how Disney+&nbsp;
will perform in a post pandemic world. Last year,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.680 --> 00:02:12.320
we actually got a small taste of what may be ahead&nbsp;
for Disney+ and it wasn’t that optimistic. If we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.320 --> 00:02:17.840
zoom back to q3 and q4 of 2021, we’ll see that&nbsp;
Disney+’s subscriber numbers barely moved from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.840 --> 00:02:24.640
116 million to 118.1 million. This means that&nbsp;
they only gained 2.1 million subscribers this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.640 --> 00:02:29.440
entire quarter which was a far cry from investor&nbsp;
expectations of 9 million new subscribers.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:33.920
But, wait a minute, why was subscriber&nbsp;
growth so slow near the end of the year?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.560 --> 00:02:39.040
Well, the thing we have to keep in mind is that&nbsp;
Disney’s fiscal quarters don’t exactly lineup with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.040 --> 00:02:44.320
the tax quarters that we’re familiar with. Our&nbsp;
first quarter is actually Disney’s second quarter,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.320 --> 00:02:49.600
our second quarter is Disney’s third quarter and&nbsp;
so on and so forth. So, when Disney reports their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.600 --> 00:02:54.160
third and fourth quarters of 2021, they’re&nbsp;
actually talking about the second and third&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.160 --> 00:02:59.120
quarters of 2021, so basically the peak of&nbsp;
summer going into fall. As you would guess,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.120 --> 00:03:03.520
this is when people are most likely to travel and&nbsp;
partake in social events given that that school&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.520 --> 00:03:08.080
is off and the weather is nice. So, it makes&nbsp;
sense that Disney’s subscriber growth slowed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.080 --> 00:03:12.880
down substantially during the summer months of&nbsp;
2021. If we look further into the graph, we’ll&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.880 --> 00:03:17.520
see that subscriber growth picked up again in the&nbsp;
winter months of 2021 and the start of 2022 when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.520 --> 00:03:22.960
Omicron was a big concern. But, as we approach the&nbsp;
summer again, investors are worried that Disney+&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.960 --> 00:03:28.240
may post subpar growth numbers once again. This&nbsp;
fear is made even worse when you consider that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.240 --> 00:03:32.800
most people haven’t really gone anywhere since the&nbsp;
pandemic started, meaning that we’ll likely see&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.800 --> 00:03:38.240
above average travel this summer. Something else&nbsp;
to consider is that many subscribers got Disney+&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.240 --> 00:03:42.640
specifically because they had nothing better to&nbsp;
do during the pandemic. But, as they return to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.640 --> 00:03:47.680
normal life, many of them are cancelling. It’s&nbsp;
also not a great sign that Netflix just posted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.680 --> 00:03:52.720
their first subscriber loss in a decade of 200,000&nbsp;
subscribers. Not to mention, they think that these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.720 --> 00:03:57.680
losses are going to get even worse as we move&nbsp;
into the summer. Now, Disney has maintained a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.680 --> 00:04:02.320
more positive long term outlook projecting that&nbsp;
they can still reach 230 million to 260 million&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.320 --> 00:04:07.920
subscribers by September of 2024. But, at this&nbsp;
point, to reach this goal, Disney would have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.920 --> 00:04:12.960
to consistently achieve 11 to 12 million new&nbsp;
subscribers every quarter. And the issue is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.960 --> 00:04:18.480
that Disney has only posted one quarter in the&nbsp;
11-12 milllion range since the end of 2020. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.480 --> 00:04:23.360
many investors are rather skeptical about Disney’s&nbsp;
projections prompoting them to leave in droves.

00:04:27.440 --> 00:04:29.360
UNDERWHELMING RECOVERY:
Alright, so it looks like Disney+&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.360 --> 00:04:34.480
is going take a hit within the next few quarters,&nbsp;
but fortunately, as we discussed at the beginning,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.480 --> 00:04:38.560
Disney is a multi-faceted business that&nbsp;
can thrive in a multitude of environments.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.120 --> 00:04:44.160
If people are going out more, that’s perfect for&nbsp;
Disney’s theme parks and their box office numbers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.160 --> 00:04:50.480
right? Well, the answer is yes and no. Disney&nbsp;
has indeed been crushing it in these sectors.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.480 --> 00:04:56.400
For example, 3 out of the 4 best opening&nbsp;
weekends of 2021 belonged to Disney. Similarly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.400 --> 00:05:00.400
Disney has been posting tremendous&nbsp;
year over year growth as high as 99%&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.400 --> 00:05:06.560
for their parks and experiences. Disney’s revenue&nbsp;
is basically back to pre pandemic levels. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.560 --> 00:05:12.480
the last quarter of 2021 was their best quarter&nbsp;
ever in terms of revenue. The only problem though&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.480 --> 00:05:17.840
is that these impressive revenue figures haven’t&nbsp;
translated to their bottom line. Their net income&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.840 --> 00:05:23.120
is only about a fifth of what it used to be pre&nbsp;
pandemic. And if we look at their net margin, this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:23.120 --> 00:05:27.200
downward trend becomes extremely clear. Over the&nbsp;
past few years, Disney’s net margin has plummeted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.200 --> 00:05:34.000
from over 20% to just 3%. And the main reason for&nbsp;
this is skyrocketing operating expenses which can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.000 --> 00:05:38.880
be traced back to two factors starting with a&nbsp;
massive staffing shortage. As you would guess,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.880 --> 00:05:43.680
Disney laid of tens of thousands of employees when&nbsp;
the pandemic rolled around, but they weren’t able&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.680 --> 00:05:48.880
to fill in these vacancies as demand recovered. In&nbsp;
fact, Disney’s total employee count has decreased&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.880 --> 00:05:53.840
for 2 years at this point. And given that demand&nbsp;
is quickly recovering to pre-pandemic levels,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.840 --> 00:05:58.640
Disney is having to increase pay substantially&nbsp;
to fill vacant positions. This issue is made even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.640 --> 00:06:04.560
worse given that 25,000 employees are actually&nbsp;
suing Disney to pay better. Now, I’m by no means&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.560 --> 00:06:10.320
trying to suggest that this battle is or is not&nbsp;
justified. But, regardless of the morality of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.320 --> 00:06:15.680
fight, increasing pay is not the best for Disney’s&nbsp;
bottom line or Disney stock. Aside from struggling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.680 --> 00:06:20.800
to find employees, Disney has also been struggling&nbsp;
against inflation. By now, I’m sure we’re all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.800 --> 00:06:25.120
well aware of how bad inflation has gotten and&nbsp;
this issue affects Disney just like the rest of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.120 --> 00:06:30.720
us. They have to pay more for supplies, food,&nbsp;
transportation, talent, energy, and so on and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.720 --> 00:06:35.520
so forth. Fortunately, Disney has great pricing&nbsp;
power when it comes to their parks and experiences&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.520 --> 00:06:40.480
meaning that they can increase prices and people&nbsp;
will still pay them. But, with that being said,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.480 --> 00:06:46.080
rising prices across the board is not the best&nbsp;
idea and for obvious reasons, Disney wants to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:46.080 --> 00:06:50.880
address this issue as strategically as possible.&nbsp;
Disney’s CFO announced that they’re looking into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.880 --> 00:06:54.880
switching suppliers, substituting products,&nbsp;
and cutting portion sizes wherever possible to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.880 --> 00:07:00.000
minimize price increases. Something else to note&nbsp;
is that while Disney may have great pricing power&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.000 --> 00:07:03.920
when it comes to their parks and experiences,&nbsp;
they don’t have that much pricing power when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.920 --> 00:07:09.280
it comes to Disney+. You see, people are often&nbsp;
ok with splurging on the occasional vacation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.280 --> 00:07:13.200
but they’re way more sensitive to price&nbsp;
increases in monthly subscriptions. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.200 --> 00:07:17.680
price sensitivity has made it extremely difficult&nbsp;
for Disney to continue doubling down on streaming&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:17.680 --> 00:07:23.120
while maintaining a low subscription cost. I do&nbsp;
believe that Disney will eventually figure it out,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:23.120 --> 00:07:29.840
but in the meantime, restaffing and&nbsp;
inflation has destroyed their net margin.

00:07:29.840 --> 00:07:31.280
GO WOKE GO BROKE:&nbsp;

00:07:31.280 --> 00:07:35.680
Aside from these fundamental issues that are&nbsp;
being reflected in Disney’s quarterly reports,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:35.680 --> 00:07:40.880
a major factor that is holding back many investors&nbsp;
from investing in Disney is their apparent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.880 --> 00:07:46.560
wokeness. I mean it’s no secret that Disney has&nbsp;
always leaned towards a more liberal perspective,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.560 --> 00:07:51.520
and this has become especially evident within&nbsp;
the past couple of years. Their movies and media&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:51.520 --> 00:07:56.400
promote LGBTQ quite openly as there’s dozens&nbsp;
of references sprinkled throughout their films.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.400 --> 00:08:00.320
This has prompted many parents to reevaluate&nbsp;
whether they want their children to be exposed to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.320 --> 00:08:04.960
such content at such an early age. Now, I’m sure&nbsp;
there’s gonna be plenty of comments down below&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.960 --> 00:08:11.120
that say “Go woke go broke”, and honestly this is&nbsp;
a pretty accurate statement and I’m not trying to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:11.120 --> 00:08:15.680
single out being woke. If Disney was leaning too&nbsp;
far to the right, it would be the same argument.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:15.680 --> 00:08:20.160
Companies should simply not be involved in&nbsp;
politics. The truth is, regardless of which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:20.160 --> 00:08:24.000
side you choose or how careful you are with&nbsp;
your stances, you’re naturally going to enrage&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.000 --> 00:08:28.960
a significant portion of the population. So, as&nbsp;
a company, it’s best to stay out of politics and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:28.960 --> 00:08:34.560
hot topics in general and just focus on running&nbsp;
your busines well. But unfortunately, Disney is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:34.560 --> 00:08:40.320
actively doing the exact opposite. For example, a&nbsp;
few months ago, Florida’s Governor, Ron Desantis,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:40.320 --> 00:08:45.040
signed a rather controversial law that many call&nbsp;
the Don’t Say Gay bill. If you’re not familiar&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:45.040 --> 00:08:49.040
with this bill, it basically outlaws classroom&nbsp;
instruction regarding gender identity between&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:49.040 --> 00:08:54.000
kindergarten through 3rd grade. Right after this&nbsp;
news came out, Disney decided that it was a great&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:54.000 --> 00:08:58.560
idea to publicly announce that they were not only&nbsp;
against this law but that it is there goal as a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:58.560 --> 00:09:04.160
company to repeal this legislation. Again, their&nbsp;
stance regarding this issue doesn’t really matter.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:04.160 --> 00:09:08.160
If they came out in full support of the bill,&nbsp;
it would’ve been just as bad for the company&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.160 --> 00:09:12.720
because investors don’t like contreversial&nbsp;
companies. From their perspective, controversy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:12.720 --> 00:09:17.600
simply threatens future growth, and at this&nbsp;
point, Disney has gone pretty far down that road.

00:09:17.600 --> 00:09:21.680
FUTURE OUTLOOK:&nbsp;

00:09:21.680 --> 00:09:26.160
At the end of the day, Disney definintely does&nbsp;
have some internal issues. They’re having to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:26.160 --> 00:09:31.280
fight against inflation and staff shortages while&nbsp;
also trying to keep prices as low as possible.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:31.280 --> 00:09:35.440
They’re also having trouble reaching some of their&nbsp;
ambitious growth targets especially with Disney+,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:36.160 --> 00:09:41.200
but honestly, I don’t think any of these are&nbsp;
a gamechanger for Disney’s overall business.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:41.200 --> 00:09:46.320
Afterall, it’s not like Disney+ is doing bad and&nbsp;
it’s not like inflation and staffing concerns&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:46.320 --> 00:09:50.640
are specific to Disney. Personally, I&nbsp;
think it’s just a matter of time until&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:50.640 --> 00:09:54.720
Disney recovers from these short term issues&nbsp;
and starts to post solid profits once again.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:55.280 --> 00:10:00.720
Something that is a gamechanger, however, is their&nbsp;
political entanglement. At this rate, it’s just a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:00.720 --> 00:10:05.280
matter of time until investors write off Disney&nbsp;
altogether as a company that’s too political to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:05.280 --> 00:10:11.040
invest in like Twitter. If you look at Twitter’s&nbsp;
revenue, for example, it’s really only gone up.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:11.040 --> 00:10:16.560
If we look at twitter stock, however, it hasn’t&nbsp;
moved in a decade. And unfortunately, this might&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:16.560 --> 00:10:21.680
be the path that Disney is going down as well. If&nbsp;
we put their political entanglemnet aside though,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:21.680 --> 00:10:26.640
Disney is looking quite nice in my opinion. In one&nbsp;
of their recent earnings calls, Disney even teased&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:26.640 --> 00:10:30.960
the idea of a Disney metaverse that is supposed to&nbsp;
connect their digital and physical offerings more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:30.960 --> 00:10:36.640
than ever before. Now, this was simply a tease&nbsp;
and we have no idea how well this plan will work&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:36.640 --> 00:10:41.600
out or if it’ll even end up happening. But, from&nbsp;
an investors perspective, it’s great to see that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:41.600 --> 00:10:46.000
Disney is looking to embrace new technology.&nbsp;
This shows that the company is still trying to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:46.000 --> 00:10:51.120
innovate and that they probably won’t stagnate&nbsp;
fundamentally anytime soon. So, personally,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:51.120 --> 00:10:56.880
I think Disney is a solid investment at $100 if&nbsp;
you’re willing to take on the political risk. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:56.880 --> 00:11:01.760
that’s just what I think and it’s not financial&nbsp;
advice. What do you guys think about Disney’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:01.760 --> 00:11:07.280
wokeness? Comment that down below. Also,&nbsp;
drop a like if Disney is nostalgic for you.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:07.280 --> 00:11:10.480
And of course, consider checking out our&nbsp;
international channels to watch our videos&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:10.480 --> 00:11:14.560
in other languages and consider subscribing&nbsp;
to see more questions logically answered.

